309 (`await updateRepoStatus(this.db, repo.path, 'ready', ...)`) sits in the start-up path, next to the re-queue predicate at :289 (`repo.status === 'indexing' && !repo.lastIndexed`). The c12 citation places `start` at index.ts:246-335, so :309 is outside fullIndex. That leaves 'indexing' at :596, then 'ready' at :726 and 'error' at :735 as the only status writes in the fullIndex range. This matches the premise's count of exactly two final writes. The ready/error pair at :726 and :735 has the success/catch shape: the ready write carries a timestamp and the error write carries undefined plus msg.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms all three anchors and confirms there is no other updateRepoStatus call inside fullIndex. The :309 'ready' write belongs to start, not fullIndex. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p15","ref":"t6","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The start-up re-queue predicate (repos left 'indexing' without lastIndexed) is at src/indexer/index.ts:289.","evidence":"The read of src/indexer/index.ts:289 returned `(repo.status === 'indexing' && !repo.lastIndexed)`. That line is the start-up re-queue predicate the premise describes: it selects repos left in 'indexing' status with no lastIndexed. The anchor resolves to the cited entity. The grep matches agree with it: S005/LLD.md:415 says \"the start-up re-queue of a repository left 'indexing' without lastIndexed is unchanged\", PLAN.md:92 says the same, and an earlier review row (PLAN.md:249) records the same read result at index.ts:289.","action":"None. The premise checks out against the evidence.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchor resolves to exactly the predicate the premise names, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p16","ref":"t6","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"repo.reindex is registered at src/daemon/index.ts:806 and only enqueues a full index.","evidence":"The anchor is confirmed. The read of src/daemon/index.ts:806 returned `'repo.reindex': async (params) => {`, and the grep for 'repo.reindex' found it registered in the daemon handler map there (src/daemon/index.ts:806). It is called from src/cli/services/repo.ts:80 as `await rpc('repo.reindex', { path: abs });`. The second half of the premise, that the handler \"only enqueues a full index\", is not covered by the evidence. Only the opening signature line was read, not the handler body, so nothing shows what the handler does: whether it only enqueues a full index, or also clears state, resets status, or runs an incremental path. The S005/PLAN.md:249 row says this anchor checked out before, but that is an earlier review's claim, not a read of the source.","action":"Read the handler body that starts at src/daemon/index.ts:806, up to the closing brace, and check that it only enqueues a full index job. Look for any side effects such as status reset, cache or graph clearing, or incremental routing. If the body does more, change t6's premise to describe what it really does before building on it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The file and line are verified, but the claim about what the handler does is not. Someone has to read the handler body before the premise is either confirmed or reworded.","options":["Confirm by reading src/daemon/index.ts:806 onward; if the body is only an enqueue of a full index, mark verified (LOW) with no edit.","If the handler does more than enqueue (e.g. resets repo status or clears index data), reword t6's premise to state the actual behavior and re-check whether t6's prescribed change still holds.","Narrow the premise to the verified part only: 'repo.reindex is registered at src/daemon/index.ts:806'. Then move the behavioral claim to an explicit task step to confirm while building."]}},{"claimId":"p17","ref":"t6","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/indexer/run-record.ts does not yet exist and a leaf module importing only types can be read by src/analyze/context/invariants.ts without importing the indexer.","evidence":"grep /run-record/ found 5 matches, and none of them is a source file at src/indexer/run-record.ts or an import of one. Three are design text: S005/PLAN.md:85, :96 and :269. One is an unrelated line in an older LLD (make-insrc-analysis-complete-used-right-E20261006b9d5c5c4/S007/LLD.md:447). The last is a test comment (src/analyze/__tests__/report-head.test.ts:11, \" * the real run-record store. No model.\"). So the module does not exist yet, as the premise says. On the layering point, grep /from '\\.\\.\\/\\.\\.\\/indexer/ shows the analyzer already imports leaf modules from src/indexer without loading the Indexer class. src/analyze/explore/item-measure.ts:17 imports makeEntityId from '../../indexer/parser/base.js', and several analyze tests import from indexer/parser too. src/analyze/context/invariants.ts does not appear in those matches, so it has no indexer import today. A new types-only leaf at src/indexer/run-record.ts, imported as '../../indexer/run-record.js', follows the same pattern. It would not pull in src/indexer/index.ts.","action":"none — verified sound. When building, keep src/indexer/run-record.ts free of runtime imports from src/indexer/index.ts or any other indexer module, so invariants.ts stays decoupled from the Indexer.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence supports the premise: the module is absent, and the analyzer already imports leaf modules from src/indexer (for example item-measure.ts:17 → indexer/parser/base.js). No artifact edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p18","ref":"t7","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"In src/analyze/context/invariants.ts: ensureNonEmptyClosure's longest-prefix lookup is at :185-217, ScopeErrorCode at :100, scopeErrorMapping at :118, TaskRefusalCode at :129 (derived from ScopeErrorCode), taskRefusalMapping at :137.","evidence":"I read every cited line, and each one matches the premise. `src/analyze/context/invariants.ts:100` is `export type ScopeErrorCode = 'scope-not-indexed' | 'scope-ref-unresolved' | 'scope-ref-kind-target-mismatch';`. `:118` is `export function scopeErrorMapping(err: unknown): ScopeErrorMapping | undefined {`. `:129` is `export type TaskRefusalCode = ScopeErrorCode | 'connection-outside-scope';`, which confirms that TaskRefusalCode is built from ScopeErrorCode. `:137` is `export function taskRefusalMapping(err: unknown): { readonly code: TaskRefusalCode; readonly message: string } | undefined {`. `:185` is `// Find the longest-prefix registered repo containing the scope.`, which is the start of the longest-prefix lookup. The end of the range (:217) was not read, but the start is correct. The grep results also show `:139` calling `scopeErrorMapping(err)` inside taskRefusalMapping, which fits the derivation.","action":"None needed. The premise checks out against the source.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"All five anchors point at the entities the premise names, so the artifact needs no edit."}},{"claimId":"p19","ref":"t7","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The plan walker calls taskRefusalMapping at src/analyze/executor/walker.ts:265.","evidence":"I read src/analyze/executor/walker.ts:265 directly. It returns `const refused = taskRefusalMapping(err);`, so the call is on the cited line. The grep agrees with that read. walker.ts:47 imports the function (`import { taskRefusalMapping, type TaskRefusalCode } from '../context/invariants.js';`). src/analyze/context/invariants.ts:137 defines it (`export function taskRefusalMapping(err: unknown): ...`). The doc comment at invariants.ts:115 also names the plan walk as a caller through `taskRefusalMapping`.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The cited file:line holds the call the premise describes, so the artifact needs no change."}},{"claimId":"p20","ref":"t7","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The registry has a 'pristine' state and statuses 'pending' / 'indexing' / 'ready' / 'error' with an errorMsg field; ScopeNotIndexedError exists.","evidence":"ScopeNotIndexedError exists: `src/analyze/context/invariants.ts:67: export class ScopeNotIndexedError extends Error {`. The status union is defined in source as `'pending' | 'indexing' | 'ready' | 'error'` at `src/shared/types.ts:696` (RegisteredRepo.status), `src/shared/types.ts:966` (RepoStatus) and `src/db/graph/codec.ts:68` (RepoStatus). `src/indexer/index.ts:287` also reads `repo.status === 'pending'`. A pristine registry is a real, tested condition. The test logs show `ok 289 - ensureNonEmptyClosure: pristine registry -> skipped silently, returns undefined`, the debug line `[analyze:context:invariants] ensureNonEmptyClosure: registry pristine; skipping invariant`, and `symbol exception: ... with a pristine registry -> ScopeNotIndexedError`. 'Pristine' means the registry has no repos registered (S005/LLD.md:116). It is not a value of the status union, and the premise does not say it is. On errorMsg: the grep hit cap of 50 was filled by doc and jetbrains-plugin matches, so no src/ line was returned. Its existence on the registry row rests on `design/indexer.html:223` (`errorMsg STRING`), `docs/plans/repo-registry-strict-contract.md:105` (`status: enum, errorMsg: string`), and the LLD's cited signature `updateRepoStatus(..., errorMsg?: string, ...) // src/db/repos.ts` (S005/LLD.md:226). These are consistent but indirect.","action":"None needed; the premise holds. As an optional check, before building t7 confirm that `errorMsg?` is on RegisteredRepo in src/shared/types.ts (around :678-698) and on RepoRow in src/db/graph/codec.ts. The capped grep did not return a src/ line for errorMsg.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms ScopeNotIndexedError at invariants.ts:67, the four-value status union in src/shared/types.ts and src/db/graph/codec.ts, and the pristine-registry skip in ensureNonEmptyClosure. errorMsg is supported by the design, plan and LLD signature documents. Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise, so no edit to the artifact is needed."}},{"claimId":"p21","ref":"t8","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"runShaper today builds the cache key at driver.ts:336, resolves scope via prepareScope at :350, reads freshness at :357, and looks up the cache at :360.","evidence":"Three of the four anchors were read and match the premise. `read src/analyze/context/driver.ts:336 → FOUND: const cacheKey: CacheKey = {` confirms the cache key is built at :336, and the grep for cacheKey also places it at driver.ts:336. `read src/analyze/context/driver.ts:350 → FOUND: const scope = await prepareScope(invocationMode, inputs, args.scopeDeps);` confirms scope is resolved through prepareScope at :350. `read src/analyze/context/driver.ts:360 → FOUND: const cached = readBundle(runId, cacheKey, opts, currentLastIndexedAt);` confirms the cache lookup at :360. No read of :357 was gathered, so the freshness-read anchor is not directly confirmed. It is indirectly supported: line 360 passes `currentLastIndexedAt`, so that value must be computed between :350 and :360, which is where the premise puts the freshness read. The order the premise states (key, then scope, then freshness, then lookup) matches the line numbers. It also agrees with the S006 code review note that \"prepareScope now runs before the bundle cache lookup\".","action":"None needed; the premise holds. If you want to be strict, read driver.ts:357 during the build to confirm `currentLastIndexedAt` is assigned there. The task's prescribed change does not depend on that exact line.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Anchors :336, :350 and :360 resolve exactly to the entities the premise names. :357 was not read, but :360 consuming currentLastIndexedAt shows the freshness value is computed in the 351–359 range. No edit is warranted."}},{"claimId":"p22","ref":"t8","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"The factory in src/analyze/context/index.ts passes caller ShapeOpts whole as runShaper's opts at three sites (:93-100, :113-120, :138-145).","evidence":"The evidence backs up the structure the premise describes, but not the specific claim it makes. The three anchors match. `src/analyze/context/index.ts:93` and `:113` each read `opts:  ShapeOpts,`, and `:138` reads the same line with one less tab. Each is a closure parameter that comes just before a `runShaper({` call: index.ts:95, :115 and :140, as the grep for /runShaper\\(/ shows. So there are three factory call sites, each with a ShapeOpts parameter in scope. What the evidence does not show is the body of any of the three `runShaper({ ... })` calls (lines 96–100, 116–120 and 141–145). So it cannot show whether `opts` is passed through unchanged (`opts` or `opts: opts`), spread, or rebuilt from chosen fields such as `{ runId: opts.runId, ... }`. The only other look at the factory body is the shaperFor snippet in docs/.../S008/.../t06.json:421, and it is cut off at `promptPath: PROMPT_PATHS[shaperId],` before the opts field. The rest of the claim, that a new ShapeOpts.scopeChecked reaches runShaper with no new pass-through, depends on exactly that unseen fact. The claim cannot be checked from this evidence, but the anchors still point at the right place.","action":"Before building t8, read src/analyze/context/index.ts:95-145 and look at the `opts` field of each of the three `runShaper({...})` calls. If all three pass `opts` unchanged or spread it, keep the premise as it is. If any of them builds a new opts object field by field, t8 must add `scopeChecked` at that site. Either way, add a unit test showing that `ShapeOpts.scopeChecked` set on `buildRunBundle` reaches `runShaper`'s `args.opts`. That test turns the premise from an assumption into something the build checks.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchors and the number of sites (three) are confirmed, but the evidence does not show how opts is forwarded. The right edit depends on index.ts:96-145, which is not in the evidence, so no find/replace edit can be safely derived. A person needs to choose between checking the claim and making the forwarding explicit.","options":["Read index.ts:95-145. If all three runShaper calls pass `opts` unchanged or spread it, keep the premise and add a unit test that ShapeOpts.scopeChecked reaches runShaper's args.opts.","Change t8 so it makes the forwarding explicit at all three sites (`opts: { ...opts }`, or `opts` passed unchanged), whatever the current form is, and add the unit test. That way a later ShapeOpts field cannot be dropped without anyone noticing.","If any site builds opts field by field, reword the premise to say t8 adds a `scopeChecked: opts.scopeChecked` pass-through at that site, and list which sites need it."]}},{"claimId":"p23","ref":"t8","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"BundleMeta in src/analyze/context/types.ts and the bundle schema meta in src/analyze/context/schema.ts exist, and validateBundleWithErrors validates bundles.","evidence":"The evidence supports each part of the premise. (1) BundleMeta exists at `src/analyze/context/types.ts:88: export interface BundleMeta {`. (2) validateBundleWithErrors is exported from src/analyze/context/schema.ts: `src/analyze/context/__tests__/bundle-report-schema.test.ts:14: import { ANALYZE_CONTEXT_BUNDLE_SCHEMA, modelFacingBundleSchema, SCHEMA_VERSION, validateBundleWithErrors } from '../schema.js';`, and the driver imports it at `src/analyze/context/driver.ts:84`. (3) It validates whole bundles, meta included: `bundle-report-schema.test.ts:33: assert.deepEqual(validateBundleWithErrors({ ...LAYERS, meta: META, report: REPORT }), { ok: true, errors: [] });` and `:48: validateBundleWithErrors({ ...LAYERS, meta: { ...META, schemaVersion: 1 } })`, which shows meta is part of the schema it checks. `schema.test.ts:106` (\"returns ok=true on a valid bundle\") and `:142`/`:151` (invalid bundles) show it accepting and rejecting bundles. Its body, recorded in the S008 measurement, compiles a validator and returns `{ ok, errors }`. One gap: the meta sub-schema inside schema.ts is not shown line by line. It is still clearly validated, because meta-carrying bundles go through validateBundleWithErrors from that module.","action":"None needed. The premise checks out against the evidence.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise checks out against the evidence. BundleMeta is at types.ts:88, validateBundleWithErrors is exported from schema.ts and the driver uses it, and the tests run bundles with a meta field through it. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p24","ref":"t9","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"In src/analyze/orchestrator/driver.ts runAnalyze has a classifier branch and a hinted-target branch, calls measureRequestScope and emits a 'classified' event, and later in the plan stage calls resolveTaskScope(intent.scopeRef, intent.target, 'the run').","evidence":"The source confirms the parts of the premise that matter for t9. `src/analyze/orchestrator/driver.ts:325` emits `emit({ type: 'classified', intent, measure });`, so the measure exists by the time the 'classified' event goes out. `src/analyze/orchestrator/driver.ts:374` calls `await resolveTaskScope(intent.scopeRef, intent.target, 'the run');`. Line 374 comes after line 325, which matches \"later in the plan stage\". The ISSUE also cites both lines from driver.ts as code: `emit({ type: 'classified', intent, measure });` at [[c1]] and `await resolveTaskScope(intent.scopeRef, intent.target, 'the run');` at [[c2]].\n\nTwo parts are supported only indirectly. The grep for /measureRequestScope/ stopped at its cap before reaching any src/ match, so no driver.ts line shows the measure call itself. It is backed by the `measure` variable passed in the line 325 event and by the S002 CR, which places `measureRequestScope` at driver.ts:317 in the classify stage. The two branches have no driver.ts line in the evidence either. The hinted branch is backed by the test file `src/analyze/orchestrator/__tests__/hinted-branch.test.ts:169-171`, which checks for the 'classified' event, and by the S002 LLD (\"After either classification branch ... the driver calls measureRequestScope\"). The earlier PLAN review row s5/t9 reached the same verdict, LOW.","action":"None. The premise is verified sound. The order is: measure, then the 'classified' event at :325, then resolveTaskScope at :374. That is the order t9 is written to change.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence directly confirms the order the task relies on: the 'classified' event at driver.ts:325 comes before resolveTaskScope(intent.scopeRef, intent.target, 'the run') at driver.ts:374. The measure call and the two branches are supported only by secondary evidence, but that gap does not change what t9 builds. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p25","ref":"t10","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts:347-348 calls the classifier then measureRequestScope with no scope check between.","evidence":"Part of the premise checks out. The read of src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts:347 found `const unsized = await classifierImpl({ input, opts });`, so the classifier call is on the cited line. Line 348 was not read. The grep for /measureRequestScope/ stopped at its 50-match cap, and every returned match is in docs/ (LLD.md, PLAN.md, CR.md, TESTS.md, .tap measurement files); none is in src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts. So nothing here shows that measureRequestScope is called on line 348, or that no scope check sits between the two calls. The only place the claim appears is the artifact's own wording in PLAN.md:125 (\"Grounding: src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts:347-348 calls the classifier and then measureRequestScope with no scope check between them\"). An earlier review row, PLAN.md:252, flagged the same citation at 344-345 as stale. The anchor now points at the right concept (the classifier call). The planned change, adding resolveTaskScope + ensureRepoQueryable after classification and before measureRequestScope in analyze.classify, does not depend on the exact line numbers. So if the cite is wrong, the work is still the same.","action":"Read src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts lines 347-350. Confirm that line 348 (or the next statement) is `measureRequestScope(unsized.scopeRef …)` / `measureRequestScope(parsed.intent.scopeRef, …)` and that no resolveTaskScope / ensureRepoQueryable / ensureNonEmptyClosure call sits between it and the classifier call. Then either keep the 347-348 range or change the premise so it does not rely on line numbers.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the classifier call at line 347. It does not confirm the measureRequestScope call at 348 or that no scope check sits between the calls. The prescribed change is the same either way, so the fix is to re-check the anchor or drop the line numbers. There is no evidence to support a different line value.","artifactEdits":[{"find":"Grounding: src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts:347-348 calls the classifier and then measureRequestScope with no scope check between them.","replace":"Grounding: src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts calls the classifier (`const unsized = await classifierImpl({ input, opts });`, line 347) and then measureRequestScope with no scope check between them."}],"options":["Re-anchor: read src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts:347-350, confirm that measureRequestScope directly follows `const unsized = await classifierImpl({ input, opts });` with no scope check between them, and keep or adjust the 347-348 range.","Re-word the premise so it does not rely on line numbers: 'src/daemon/analyze-rpc.ts calls classifierImpl and then measureRequestScope with no scope check (resolveTaskScope / ensureRepoQueryable) between them.'"]}},{"claimId":"p26","ref":"t11","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"The graph-reading search requests in src/daemon/index.ts are exactly twelve: search.callers, search.callees, search.callers_nhop, search.by_file, search.closure, entity.summary, entity.closure, entity.unreachable, repo.depends_on, repo.search_cross_repo, locate.inferParents, search.query.","evidence":"The source tree directly confirms 6 of the 12 named requests as handlers in src/daemon/index.ts: 'locate.inferParents' at :1081, 'entity.summary' at :1102, 'entity.closure' at :1107, 'entity.unreachable' at :1123, 'repo.depends_on' at :1184 and 'repo.search_cross_repo' at :1203. The other six (search.query, search.closure, search.callers, search.callees, search.by_file, search.callers_nhop) were never seen in src/daemon/index.ts. The grep for /'search\\.[a-z_]+'/ hit its 50-match cap, and the matches it did return all come from docs, src/analyze tests and the analyze 'search.text' exploration type. The only support for those six is the artifact's own PLAN.md table (lines 253 and 274), which claims search.query :1048, search.closure :1060, search.callers :1065, search.callees :1070 and search.by_file :1216. The search.callers_nhop citation is cut off there. That table is self-referential and does not count as ground truth. The word \"exactly\" is also unchecked: none of the probes listed every handler key in src/daemon/index.ts, so other graph-reading requests may exist that are not on the list. Nothing here contradicts the premise. Half of it is confirmed and the rest, including the claim that the list is complete, cannot be checked from this evidence.","action":"Re-run the probe on src/daemon/index.ts alone with no truncation, for example `rg -n \"^\\s*'(search|entity|repo|locate)\\.[A-Za-z_]+'\\s*:\" src/daemon/index.ts`. Check that all six search.* handlers exist at their cited lines. Then list every handler that reads the graph store to confirm there is no 13th graph-reading request. If any are missing or extra, update the t11 contract-test inventory and t12's guarded set of 11 to match.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Six of the twelve names are confirmed against source. The six search.* handlers, and the claim that there are exactly twelve, cannot be checked because the search grep was truncated before it reached src/daemon/index.ts. A person needs to re-run a probe limited to that file before the inventory gets pinned in a contract test.","options":["Re-probe src/daemon/index.ts on its own and keep the list of 12 as written if all six search.* handlers turn up and no other graph-reading handler exists","If more graph-reading handlers turn up, add them to the t11 inventory and to t12's guarded set, and update the count","Reword the premise from 'exactly twelve' to 'at least these twelve', and have the t11 test pin the list it actually enumerates from the handler table instead of a hard-coded count"]}},{"claimId":"p27","ref":"t12","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"Handler lines and params in src/daemon/index.ts: search.query :1048 (none), search.closure :1060 {repoPath}, search.callers :1065, search.callees :1070 {entityId}, locate.inferParents :1081 {repoPath}, entity.summary :1102 {entityId}, entity.closure :1107 and entity.unreachable :1123 {rootIds}, repo.depends_on :1184 and repo.search_cross_repo :1203 {repoId}, search.by_file :1216 {filePath}, search.callers_nhop :1221 {entityId}.","evidence":"All 12 cited line anchors resolve to the named handlers in src/daemon/index.ts: :1048 'search.query', :1060 'search.closure', :1065 'search.callers', :1070 'search.callees', :1081 'locate.inferParents', :1102 'entity.summary', :1107 'entity.closure', :1123 'entity.unreachable', :1184 'repo.depends_on', :1203 'repo.search_cross_repo', :1216 'search.by_file', :1221 'search.callers_nhop'. Each read returned only the handler's opening line (`'<name>': async (params) => {`). None of the gathered evidence shows the handler bodies, so the parameter names the premise assigns ({repoPath}, {entityId}, {rootIds}, {repoId}, {filePath}, and \"none\" for search.query) can't be confirmed or refuted. The premise also lists params for some handlers only by grouping (search.callers shares {entityId} with search.callees, entity.closure shares {rootIds} with entity.unreachable, repo.depends_on shares {repoId} with repo.search_cross_repo). The PLAN.md row s5/t12 states these params per handler, and they agree with the grouping.","action":"The line anchors need no change. Before building t12's guard, read the body of each handler at the cited lines and check which params field it actually reads, for example `params.repoPath` versus `params.repo`, and `params.rootIds` versus `params.ids`. Fix any param name that doesn't match the code. If a param name is wrong, the guard would read a field that is always undefined and could never fire, or would always refuse.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The handler line citations are confirmed. The per-handler param names are what the guard depends on, and the evidence doesn't cover them. The evidence gives no value to substitute, so no automatic edit is safe. Someone has to read the handler bodies at the cited lines to confirm the params.","options":["Read the handler bodies at :1048 to :1225 and confirm each params field name. Keep the premise if they all match.","If any handler reads a different field, correct the param name in the premise and in t12's guard spec before building."]}},{"claimId":"p28","ref":"t12","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"The only other graph-reading daemon requests deliberately outside the guard are repo.stats (index.ts:588), codeReview.grounding (:762), repo.entitiesForSourceRoots (source-roots-rpc.ts:21), and analyze.*; the workflow locate path (src/workflow/locate/infer-daemon.ts:65) calls resolveClosure itself.","evidence":"All four anchors check out. src/daemon/index.ts:588 is `'repo.stats': async (params) => {`. src/daemon/index.ts:762 is `'codeReview.grounding': async (params) => {`. src/daemon/source-roots-rpc.ts:21 is `export const METHOD_ENTITIES_FOR_SOURCE_ROOTS = 'repo.entitiesForSourceRoots';`. src/workflow/locate/infer-daemon.ts:65 is `const closure = await resolveClosure(db, repoPath);`, so the locate path does call resolveClosure itself.\n\nThe premise also claims the list is complete (\"the only other\"), and the evidence cannot confirm that. The grep for resolveClosure( finds three more graph-reading call sites in the daemon:\n- index.ts:1062 (`return resolveClosure(db, repoPath);`, the search.closure handler)\n- index.ts:1186 (`const closurePaths = await resolveClosure(db, repoId);`)\n- index.ts:1209 (`const closurePaths = await resolveClosure(db, repoId);`)\n\nThe evidence does not show whether these sit inside the guard. The S011 LLD row also names other daemon handlers that reach graph-derived data: 'codeReview.freshness' (:772), 'codeReview.run' (:1877, a stream handler that wraps the grounding assembler), 'repo.resolveForCwd' (:615) and 'workflow.review' (:1884). The premise lists none of them, and the evidence does not show whether they are guarded or deliberately left out. A previous review of this same story (S005/PLAN.md:245, s5/t12) already flagged this kind of list as HIGH for leaving out graph-reading handlers. So each listed member is real, but the evidence cannot show the list is complete.","action":"Check every handler in index.ts that reaches the graph store (getGraphStore, resolveClosure, or assembleCodeReviewGrounding). That covers the plain handlers and the stream handlers, including the ones at :1062, :1186, :1209, :615, :772, :1877 and :1884. Mark each one as either inside the guard (part of the twelve) or deliberately outside it, and add any that are missing to the exclusion list. Without that, someone building t12 could leave one of these handlers unguarded without knowing it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"All four named anchors exist, but the evidence shows more graph-reading call sites (resolveClosure at index.ts:1062/:1186/:1209, plus codeReview.run/freshness, repo.resolveForCwd and workflow.review) whose guard status the premise does not cover. Someone has to sort each one as guarded or excluded before the list can be called complete.","options":["Enumerate every graph-reading handler in index.ts (including those at :1062, :1186, :1209, :772, :1877) and confirm that each is in the guarded twelve or added to this exclusion list","Drop the word 'only' and describe the list as the known exclusions, then add a source-scan test asserting that every getGraphStore/resolveClosure caller in index.ts is either guarded or on an explicit allowlist","Bring codeReview.freshness / codeReview.run / repo.resolveForCwd / workflow.review under the guard if they read graph data that should be index-consistent"]}},{"claimId":"p29","ref":"t13","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"search.closure goes through resolveClosure and repo.depends_on / repo.search_cross_repo go through the DEPENDS_ON closure; the cross-repo result set comprises seven requests plus search.query.","evidence":"The routing part of the premise checks out. src/daemon/index.ts:1060-1062 is the `'search.closure'` handler, and it returns `resolveClosure(db, repoPath)`. The calls at src/daemon/index.ts:1186 and :1209 (`const closurePaths = await resolveClosure(db, repoId);`) sit inside the `'repo.depends_on'` and `'repo.search_cross_repo'` handlers. resolveClosure is defined at src/db/search.ts:65 and documents itself as 'transitive DEPENDS_ON repos from a root repo' (search.ts:6). So filtering at these points does affect the DEPENDS_ON closure.\n\nThe count is wrong. PLAN.md:150 and LLD.md:204 list the cross-repo requests as search.callers, search.callees, search.callers_nhop, entity.closure, entity.unreachable, search.closure, repo.depends_on and repo.search_cross_repo. That is eight requests plus search.query, not seven.\n\nThe count error does not change what gets built, because the list of handlers t13 has to filter is spelled out in the plan. There is also a fourth caller of resolveClosure at src/workflow/locate/infer-daemon.ts:65. A filter placed only at the handler level would not cover it, which matches the open design point already noted at PLAN.md:351-353.","action":"Change 'seven requests plus search.query' to 'eight requests plus search.query', and list all eight so none is missed: search.callers, search.callees, search.callers_nhop, entity.closure, entity.unreachable, search.closure, repo.depends_on, repo.search_cross_repo. Separately, decide whether the under-index filter goes inside resolveClosure (src/db/search.ts:65), which would also cover infer-daemon.ts:65, or stays at the three daemon call sites (index.ts:1062/1186/1209).","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The routing claim checks out against the code: the three daemon handlers call resolveClosure, and resolveClosure follows DEPENDS_ON. Only the count is wrong: the evidence lists eight cross-repo requests, not seven. No artifact edit is included because the exact 'seven requests' wording could not be found verbatim in the artifact file on disk (PLAN.md was not present at the expected path), so a find/replace edit could fail or hit the wrong text.","options":["Correct the count to 'eight requests plus search.query' and list all eight handlers explicitly","Drop the numeric count and refer to the explicit handler list in PLAN.md:150 instead","As well as fixing the count, move the under-index filtering into resolveClosure (src/db/search.ts:65) so the infer-daemon.ts:65 caller is covered too"]}},{"claimId":"p30","ref":"t14","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/daemon/tools/builtins/graph/index.ts defines graph_entity (:73), graph_search (:113), graph_callers and graph_callees from one factory (:157-167), and graph_query (:308) — five graph tools.","evidence":"All four anchors resolve to what the premise names. `read src/daemon/tools/builtins/graph/index.ts:73` → `id: 'graph_entity',`; `:113` → `id: 'graph_search',`; `:157` → `// graph_callers / graph_callees`, the header comment of the shared factory block the premise cites as :157-167; `:308` → `id: 'graph_query',`. So three tool ids are written out as literals, and the callers/callees pair is set up in one commented factory block, which fits the premise's 'from one factory' wording. That makes five tools. The grep /graph_(entity|search|callers|callees|query)/ was cut off at 50 matches, all of them in docs/ (LLD.md:254, PLAN.md:36/159/215, and the S002/S007 test baselines that show graph_query ops). It adds nothing against the premise. PLAN.md:246 records an earlier closed-union finding that found no literal 'graph_callers'/'graph_callees' ids. That fits ids the factory builds rather than writes out as literals, and the comment at :157 confirms the factory exists. PLAN.md:276 also records an earlier pass on this same citation that judged it LOW.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Every cited anchor resolves to the entity the premise names, and the :157 comment confirms the callers/callees factory. No correction is needed. The earlier closed-union finding at PLAN.md:246 (that the tools are 'exactly three') looks like it came from a grep for quoted literals, which cannot see ids the factory builds. Before anyone applies its 'three tools' edit, they should read :157-167 to confirm the ids that block generates."}},{"claimId":"p31","ref":"t15","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"step.call is registered at src/daemon/index.ts:1902 and analyze.context.buildRun at :1740; src/daemon/steps/host.ts maps insrc_analyze to runAnalyzeOneShot and insrc_analyze_step to handleAnalyzeStep.","evidence":"All four parts of the claim match the source. The read of src/daemon/index.ts:1902 returns `'step.call': async (params, send, signal) => {`, and the read of src/daemon/index.ts:1740 returns `'analyze.context.buildRun': async (params) => {`. The step table in src/daemon/steps/host.ts maps both analyze tools as stated: line 64 has `insrc_analyze: (args) => runAnalyzeOneShot(args),` (imported at host.ts:23 from './analyze-oneshot.js'), and line 65 has `insrc_analyze_step: (args) => handleAnalyzeStep(args),` (imported at host.ts:24 from './analyze-step/handler.js'). The definitions exist at src/daemon/steps/analyze-oneshot.ts:83 (`export async function runAnalyzeOneShot(`) and src/daemon/steps/analyze-step/handler.ts:56 (`export async function handleAnalyzeStep`). The MCP side calls 'step.call' from src/mcp/forward-step.ts:119, so the method is reachable.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Every anchor points at the claimed entity. No edit needed."}},{"claimId":"p32","ref":"t16","kind":"ordering","severity":"MED","premise":"Task dependency chain holds: t2←t1, t3←t2, t4←t3, t6←t5, t7←t4,t6, t8←t7, t9←t8, t10←t7, t12←t7,t11, t13←t12, t14←t12,t13, t15←t9,t10, t16←all; t11 precedes the guard (decision q4e48e4a6) and the plan has exactly 16 tasks.","evidence":"The gathered evidence covers only part of the chain. Nothing in it contradicts the premise. Confirmed: PLAN.md:29 lists `t7` with dependencies `t4`, `t6`, which matches t7←t4,t6. PLAN.md:30 lists `t8` with dependency `t7`, which matches t8←t7. PLAN.md:159 (the graph-tools task) says \"Reuses the guard of t12 and the filtering of t13\", which fits t14←t12,t13. Not covered: the evidence has no task-table rows for t1–t6 or t9–t16. Nothing shows the dependencies of t2, t3, t4, t6, t9, t10, t12, t13, t15 or t16 (←all). Nothing shows where t11 sits relative to the guard, and the evidence never mentions decision q4e48e4a6. The task count of exactly 16 is also not shown. The greps were for ensureRepoQueryable and RepoIndexingError, so they only touched the t7/t8/t14 rows and do not test the ordering claim as a whole.","action":"Re-run the probe against the PLAN.md task table (the `| n | **`tN`** … | deps |` rows) for every task t1–t16. Confirm each dependency edge, that t16 depends on all of t1–t15, and that the table has exactly 16 rows. Grep PLAN.md and LLD.md for q4e48e4a6 and confirm t11 (the indexer run record) is ordered before the guard tasks t7 and t12. Until that is done, treat the premise as partly verified: the t7, t8 and t14 edges hold, and the rest is unverifiable.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise, and the edges it does show (t7←t4,t6; t8←t7; t14←t12,t13) match. The remaining edges, the t11/q4e48e4a6 ordering and the 16-task count are not in the evidence, so no edit can be derived from it. The fix is to verify further, not to change the artifact.","options":["Re-probe the PLAN.md dependency table rows t1–t16 and the q4e48e4a6 decision, then re-judge","Accept with a note: the dependency edges shown are consistent, and the rest of the chain is to be checked when the plan is approved"]}},{"claimId":"p33","ref":"t1","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"The Ollama JS client surfaces transport failures as Error('connect ECONNREFUSED'), 'socket hang up', TypeError('fetch failed'), TypeError('terminated'), or rejections during for-await stream iteration.","evidence":"None of the evidence comes from the `ollama` client library, so this premise cannot be checked against the library itself.\n- grep /from 'ollama'/ found only the two places that use the client: src/agent/providers/ollama.ts:1 and src/indexer/embedder.ts:1, both `import { Ollama } from 'ollama';`. No library source was searched.\n- grep /ECONNREFUSED|fetch failed|socket hang up/ hit the 50-match cap. Every match shown is in docs/epics/** and comes from this project's own documents and test output:\n  - S005/PLAN.md:45 describes a stand-in client that throws Error('connect ECONNREFUSED'), Error('socket hang up'), TypeError('fetch failed') or TypeError('terminated').\n  - The baseline test output (analyze.txt:199/202/205) feeds those strings into classifyOllamaError.\n  - S006/CR.md:43 says that undici/fetch raise `TypeError: fetch failed` and `TypeError: terminated`.\n- These lines show what the project assumes, not what the library guarantees. S005/PLAN.md:255 records the same UNVERIFIABLE verdict for this premise from an earlier review.\n- This is MED, not HIGH. Task t1 wraps every failure from the request call or from stream iteration as ModelCallFailedError at the call site. That change is still correct even if the library's exact error texts or types differ from the list, so no build-breaking failure follows.","action":"Reword the premise so it no longer depends on the library's exact error strings. It should say that any rejection from the Ollama client's request call or from async iteration of its response stream counts as a transport failure, wrapped as ModelCallFailedError by call site. Keep the listed texts only as example inputs for the stand-in client in the tests. Alternatively, confirm the texts by reading node_modules/ollama (its fetch/undici path) and cite those lines.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence cannot confirm the library's error texts. The prescribed change, wrapping errors at the call site, does not depend on them, so rewording the premise around call-site wrapping makes it verifiable without changing what gets built. No artifactEdits are given because the evidence does not show the premise's exact wording in the artifact.","options":["Reword t1's premise to: 'Every failure the Ollama client raises from the request call or from async iteration of its response stream (such as ECONNREFUSED, socket hang up, fetch failed or terminated) is a transport failure, wrapped as ModelCallFailedError by call site and not matched by message text.' (the same wording as the option at S005/PLAN.md:332)","Keep the premise but add a citation from node_modules/ollama (its fetch/stream code) and undici showing the listed error types and texts","Downgrade the premise to a test-fixture assumption: the listed texts are only the inputs the stand-in client throws, and nothing claims that the real library produces exactly these"]}},{"claimId":"p34","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"MED","premise":"Every LLD s5 test-strategy item (32 rows) is covered by a plan task, and every task traces to LLD refs c1-c14; the plan ships with no store migration and no re-index (LLD migration step 5).","evidence":"The evidence supports part of the premise. On no migration and no re-index: LLD.md:276 says lastRun is optional and that \"No key, kind byte or existing field changes, and no migrati[on]\", and that an older row decodes with lastRun undefined. PLAN.md:175 (t16) says \"restart it (no store migration, no re-index)\". The earlier review row at PLAN.md:277 (s5/t16, LOW) confirms decodeRepoRow at src/db/graph/codec.ts:114 handles older rows. On traceability: the matched tasks cite refs inside c1–c14. t5 cites [[c9]] (PLAN.md:27), t16 cites [[c14]] (PLAN.md:38), and the refs c6 and c9 are defined at PLAN.md:225 and :228. On coverage: the lastRun-related LLD s5 test subjects map to plan tasks in the PLAN coverage table. PLAN.md:188 and :200 map to t7, :196 and :202 to t5, and :210 to t6. Each matches LLD test subjects at LLD.md:425, :428 and :438. The probe was a single grep for /lastRun/, so it covers only the lastRun rows. It cannot confirm the claimed total of 32 LLD s5 test-strategy rows, that every one of them has a covering task, or that every task t1–t16 traces into c1–c14. Rows and tasks not about lastRun (for example t1–t4 and t8–t15) were not in the evidence. The count and the \"every\" claims are therefore UNVERIFIABLE from what was gathered. Nothing in the evidence contradicts them.","action":"Re-run the check with probes that cover everything, not just lastRun. (1) Count the rows in the LLD s5 test-strategy / acceptance table and the rows in the PLAN.md coverage table that starts around line 186, and confirm both are 32. (2) Check that each LLD test subject appears verbatim in a PLAN coverage row with a non-empty task. (3) Grep the PLAN task table, lines 23–38, for the [[cN]] ref of each task t1–t16, and confirm all fall in c1–c14 and that the refs list, around line 220, defines exactly c1–c14. The no-migration / no-re-index part needs no action.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The no-migration / no-re-index claim and the lastRun slice of the coverage claim are confirmed. The 32-row count and the 'every row / every task' claims were not addressed by the lastRun-only grep. The premise needs either a fuller probe or softer wording, so there is no evidence-derived text edit to make.","options":["Re-probe: count the LLD s5 test-strategy rows and the PLAN coverage-table rows, and grep each task's [[cN]] refs, to confirm 32 rows and c1–c14 traceability. Keep the premise if they check out.","Reword the premise to drop the exact count (for example 'every LLD s5 test-strategy item is covered by a plan task') until the 32 has been counted against the tables.","Accept with a note: the lastRun rows (t5/t6/t7/t16) are verified covered and traced, and no migration or re-index is confirmed. Full-table coverage still needs a manual check before approval."]}}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":13,"low":21},"reviewedAt":"2026-10-13T11:07:09.420Z","model":"cli-claude:opus","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
